 ## Overview
-Occlusion Clipping with Orthogonal Opacity Map is an acceleration technique for texture-based volume rendering that skips occluded voxels by creating a low-resolution opacity map on a plane orthogonal to a major dataset axis [S1]. It solves the problem of inefficient per-voxel occlusion testing in texture-based rendering by operating at the sub-volume level, culling fully hidden sub-volumes and clipping partially hidden ones [S1]. Unlike early ray termination which tests occlusion per-voxel, this method tests occlusion at the sub-volume level, which is more efficient for texture-based rendering where primitives contain thousands of voxels [S1]. It differs from hierarchical approaches like [Hidden Volume Removal using Hierarchical Occlusion Maps](2624-hidden-volume-removal-using-hierarchical-occlusion-maps.md) by using a fixed-resolution, multi-pass averaging strategy rather than a tree structure, and it complements [Empty Space Skipping via Data Structures](5619-empty-space-skipping-via-data-structures.md) by being more effective when there are many non-empty voxels and significant occlusion [S1].
