 ### Occlusion Culling for Point Splatting
-A technique to save time by culling points that are occluded by opaque volume material in front of their footprint. It requires front-to-back rendering to maintain an opacity image. Mueller '00 method: After compositing, convolve the opacity image with a box filter sized to the projected footprint. If a pixel value in the convolved map is $>$ threshold, the entire footprint neighborhood is $>$ threshold, allowing fast culling by checking only the pixel nearest to the projected point center [S2, unofficial]. Lee '00 method: Keep points in an octree and maintain a visibility map as a quadtree. Check the projection of an octree node against the corresponding level of the visibility map quadtree. Cull occluded octree nodes; subdivide if not occluded; rasterize points that fail the occlusion test [S2, unofficial].
